"t":624.0},"charspan":[0,97],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"With the help of Lemma 2, this term can be bounded in the desired way; which concludes the proof:"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Remark The restriction to 8 < 1 in Theorem 3 was made just for simplicity. If the source term has higher spatial regularity; and if further conditions of the type (15) are fulfilled , then we can also show higher  temporal order of convergence: The additional conditions can be derived by expanding the defect in (16) even further, and it can be shown as in Lemma 3 that are implied by the underlying quadrature rule being of higher order: In particular; full (classical) order is achieved for sufficiently smooth source term with periodic boundary conditions. they","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":466.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":90.0,"r":489.0,"t":582.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,565],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"Remark The restriction to 8 < 1 in Theorem 3 was made just for simplicity. If the source term has higher spatial regularity; and if further conditions of the type (15) are fulfilled , then we can also show higher  temporal order of convergence: The additional conditions can be derived by expanding the defect in (16) even further, and it can be shown as in Lemma 3 that are implied by the underlying quadrature rule being of higher order: In particular; full (classical) order is achieved for sufficiently smooth source term with periodic boundary conditions. they"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Example To illustrate the sharpness of the bounds in Theorem 3, we consider the linear parabolic problem","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":424.6666666666667,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":90.0,"r":488.6666666666667,"t":454.6666666666667},"charspan":[0,104],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"Example To illustrate the sharpness of the bounds in Theorem 3,